        {
            "number": 3,
            "revision": "571fac705017a1cfa468d1cbc97c8ddb8c0672ee",
            "parents": [
                "64b85f108ffe3b7b5338974ff7ec6e2b6d92f977"
            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/92/88492/3",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "Ed Tanous",
                "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                "username": "edtanous"
            },
            "createdOn": 1774025380,
            "author": {
                "name": "Ed Tanous",
                "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                "username": "edtanous"
            },
            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Adrian Ambro\u017cewicz",
                        "email": "adrian.ambrozewicz@gmail.com",
                        "username": "aambroze"
                    },
                    "message": "RAII helper seems useful, as it creates predictable behavior. Limited adoption might come from the fact that:\n- services don't properly remove_interface, but they don't destroy objects unless application ends, so that's no problem\n- they do it \"old way\", because it's what majority of the code uses\n\nI wonder - what is the trouble with carrying this implementation forward? What new implementation will be hard, because of this inconsistency?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "> RAII helper seems useful, as it creates predictable behavior.\n\nWhat does this mean specifically versus the remove_interface in the destructor?\n\nI agree, RAII overall is useful, and that's the goal, but this version that accepts an arbitrary factory/callable is difficult to manage.\n\n> Limited adoption might come from the fact that:\n> - services don't properly remove_interface, but they don't destroy objects unless application ends, so that's no problem\n\n?  Every application I know of uses remove_interface.  Can you give examples that don't?\n\n> - they do it \"old way\", because it's what majority of the code uses\n\nNew code also seems to do it the \"old\" way, so that doesn't really track for me.\n\n> \n> I wonder - what is the trouble with carrying this implementation forward? What new implementation will be hard, because of this inconsistency?\n\nI'd like to see an RAII class for this stuff, but because of the callback, this API isn't something I can propagate through the whole codebase.  Initially removing it so we can add another RAII object that would work for the general use case I think is a better overall goal.  Also keeps us consistent for now so we're not inventing a 3rd way to do this before deprecating one.\n\nThe overall goal is to deprecate remove_interface, functionally by making it do nothing."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Adrian Ambro\u017cewicz",
                        "email": "adrian.ambrozewicz@gmail.com",
                        "username": "aambroze"
                    },
                    "message": ">> services don't properly remove_interface, but they don't destroy objects unless application ends, so that's no problem\n\n> ? Every application I know of uses `remove_interface()`. Can you give examples that don't?\n\nI literally picked one of the matches for `add_interface()` at random and found example first try: https://github.com/openbmc/phosphor-u-boot-env-mgr/blob/master/src/u-boot-env-mgr.cpp\n\nIt doesn't prove anything though. Just confirmed the hunch. I suspect it's okay, as interface is removed when the bus is destroyed.\n\n>> they do it \"old way\", because it's what majority of the code uses\n\n> New code also seems to do it the \"old\" way, so that doesn't really track for me.\n\nSure, perhaps no adoption comes from the fact, that the new API is just not known. I can see lot of destructors defined only for the sole purpose of calling `remove_interface()` bunch of times. In my experience, necessity of defining destructor just to free up resources, is not welcome with greatest enthusiasm. It's just yet another boilerplate code and source of errors I wish i didn't have to write.\n\nAll in all - I'm not gatekeeping anything. I suspect this API had it's profits and merit, and limited adoption doesn't mean it's bad. Limited adoption makes it easier to drop it though. If it comes in your way I understand your sentiment.\n\nIf end goal is to make `remove_interface()` obsolete, I won't mind how it's achieved, as this is step in the right direction."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "> >> services don't properly remove_interface, but they don't destroy objects unless application ends, so that's no problem\n> \n> > ? Every application I know of uses `remove_interface()`. Can you give examples that don't?\n> \n> I literally picked one of the matches for `add_interface()` at random and found example first try: https://github.com/openbmc/phosphor-u-boot-env-mgr/blob/master/src/u-boot-env-mgr.cpp\n\nPoint taken.  I do see quite a few with a grep, but my intent is that I'd like to get those cleaned up with RAII, but keeping this separate class working with the callback is slowing that down.\n\n> \n> It doesn't prove anything though. Just confirmed the hunch. I suspect it's okay, as interface is removed when the bus is destroyed.\n> \n> >> they do it \"old way\", because it's what majority of the code uses\n> \n> > New code also seems to do it the \"old\" way, so that doesn't really track for me.\n> \n> Sure, perhaps no adoption comes from the fact, that the new API is just not known. I can see lot of destructors defined only for the sole purpose of calling `remove_interface()` bunch of times. In my experience, necessity of defining destructor just to free up resources, is not welcome with greatest enthusiasm. It's just yet another boilerplate code and source of errors I wish i didn't have to write.\n\nI don't really want to theorize here.  The point is that this API has existed for a very long time and even in new code isn't used.  If we want consistency, we can:\n1. try to get all repos to take patches to make_unique_interface.\n2. try to make dbus_interface itself an RAII\n\nThe later is what I'm trying to accomplish, but this method stands in the way.\n\n> \n> All in all - I'm not gatekeeping anything. I suspect this API had it's profits and merit, and limited adoption doesn't mean it's bad. Limited adoption makes it easier to drop it though. If it comes in your way I understand your sentiment.\n> \n> If end goal is to make `remove_interface()` obsolete, I won't mind how it's achieved, as this is step in the right direction.\n\n\ud83d\udc4d. Sounds like we're on the same page.  Ultimately, I want to turn remove_interface into a NOOP call, then we can start cleaning up over time.  Sounds like we closed this out.  I appreciate the discussion with you."
                }
            ],
